#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217980} :activity/id #uuid "f67186a8-3fe3-5bdd-b09e-4edfe15e8cef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049837 :topic/id #uuid "17cac727-fab1-5837-8069-a3e9364662a5" :topic/math-academy-id 6341} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218320 :tutorial/content "Consider the line $y = x + 2$ and the parabola $y = x^2 + x + 1,$ shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/9d/9d7ca10163c9b5096f90e19bdb50d98881bd55e92cbaea4aa5af6696e728f9f3.png)\n\nWe can see from the diagram that there are two points of intersection. So let's calculate the coordinates of these intersection points.\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations $y = x + 2$ and $y = x^2 + x + 1.$ In other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+1 \\\\ y=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$ using our knowledge of quadratic equations, we get\n![](images/96/9618f0221da97c281424def31e5d2a8fb96422b800928ad4ec4a01ed4dbad3d7.svg)\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=−1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,1).$\n\n- Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(1)+2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection are $\\left(-1,\\,1 \\right)$ and $\\left(1,\\,3 \\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b907a9fb-d730-5d33-b47d-e448e4bc64dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13862 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a89077dd-e912-5270-b496-6fe6281d817b" :step/math-academy-id 509403 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216959 :question/id #uuid "44ed3aff-8a4b-520c-808f-9e3ff928ac74" :question/math-academy-id "e-19814"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31b2bec9-3dff-557c-8cf5-a54f145fc45c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050984}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532707}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "1e5979d0-7029-563e-b231-e335fadaa16d" :step/math-academy-id 509404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216960 :question/id #uuid "9aa14c81-aeaa-51b7-9d16-468e22bc113d" :question/math-academy-id "e-19815"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570733}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "67ccd293-c59d-59e8-a1d5-686a88bbfc0e" :step/math-academy-id 509408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218321 :tutorial/content "![](images/b9/b9cb25bbb7b3076a3dae8688a232d18ec4092d68d9e815dedb438bc6900d97bb.png)\n\nA line and a parabola may have **two**, \n**one**, or **no** points of intersection.\n\nThis is similar to how a quadratic equation may have  **two**, \n**one**, or **no** solutions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "befa8bb3-607a-57e3-b906-28765ffb7779" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13863 :tutorial/title "The Number of Intersection Points"} :step/id #uuid "5955a5e4-10f4-59b2-8eb7-8c63323e0526" :step/math-academy-id 509412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216961 :question/id #uuid "b24d4adc-8623-5fa9-9ac3-f95d7b4c0d1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-19816"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570728}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "d1bb51f4-11dc-57e8-988f-57c86f8a0159" :step/math-academy-id 509413 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216962 :question/id #uuid "b2ead57b-8fd7-5d98-93fe-0e06290a5aa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-19817"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050503}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570743}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"} :step/id #uuid "3812edf3-d3fb-5828-8887-e0cd46b17f0a" :step/math-academy-id 509417}] :activity/title "Finding Intersections of Lines and Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186217314}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548150} :activity/id #uuid "ab768da5-a0bb-5051-adca-1563f211c382" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049837 :topic/id #uuid "17cac727-fab1-5837-8069-a3e9364662a5" :topic/math-academy-id 6341} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "340c4803-d8c0-59d9-a251-82d6850f70ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547423} {:answer/id #uuid "3a39b58c-130d-5ebb-836a-1129846e881e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k>\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547440} {:answer/id #uuid "5757b43b-f6e1-569c-b37a-4f6109bc720f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547511} {:answer/id #uuid "87f5df3e-d889-51a5-9bbf-55a47147c1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k<\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547611} {:answer/id #uuid "9106e55b-9dcf-53a3-80ef-e117f396ee65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k<\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547639} :answer-field/id #uuid "83300691-a442-5ef8-b2cc-b11ca96f7be8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "95764a8d-23d1-5507-836a-2817991f65d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152314" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y=x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & =x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+1-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=-1,$ and $c=1-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-1)\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{1-4+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{4k-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k-3 & <0 \\\\ 4k & <3 \\\\ k & <\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<\\frac{3}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "12bd621a-7000-5626-840e-1e2db112402f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548183}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ee5989-15d3-5396-8ca7-f4be1dd32b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547527} :answer-field/id #uuid "70af0a0d-0f7f-5758-a36e-34036e71a3d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6296c1c8-c2d4-5228-a381-4cbde8c22806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547530} :answer-field/id #uuid "88ead4fe-b75d-5d94-a601-d98ef461e331" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28de8699-3497-5296-9483-734e67042dfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-250220" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+x+4$ and the line $y=5x$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+4 \\\\ y=5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+4 & =5x \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=2.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x \\\\  & =5(2) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only point of intersection is $(2,10).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/268de319ae56302e9b633e09a4c74a0ebd62f3d8ed47829f709036082a644bf4.png)"} :step/id #uuid "6226e994-a854-5980-aad5-8e17608603fe"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e632f5-c50c-5da4-bb1c-9c0ced37d622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547363} :answer-field/id #uuid "1917052b-8eb2-50f9-8460-330446612d70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db2c4ef0-9137-54be-b391-5354ceba0e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547778}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547778} :answer-field/id #uuid "db3f8322-9a29-5bb3-823b-39d461179c3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fc9c836c-676e-5aa5-b4fe-1701bb866c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-250103" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+1$ and the line $y=3x$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x+1 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+1 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}+5x+1-3x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-1.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x \\\\  & =3(-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only point of intersection is $(-1,-3).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a5db47f31fc3e7be1afa4c5413e193c0751b68998e573093c69da5f2441b5a7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "b38605e4-4f03-5b1a-b27c-afee22c2d31c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548114}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cc47893-4576-56e2-b51b-ce99eaf337e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547490} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2b5802-b097-565d-8a8d-3cbc86eb5664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547496} {:answer/id #uuid "bba43083-7060-5fde-819c-d1d64e90ed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547717} {:answer/id #uuid "cabe3545-1601-5fc2-b776-2d1f2769c466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547748} {:answer/id #uuid "fe61a9af-bb8a-5a5e-a47a-8216aa26ccd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547717} :answer-field/id #uuid "87155073-92f3-57b6-9c30-b010bce3c156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8dc89317-431f-536c-84f1-f80a52b02432" :question/math-academy-id "q-152279" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x+2$ and the line $y=2-2x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x+2 \\\\ y=2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+2 & =2-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ x(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=0.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2-2{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2-2(-4) \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,10).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2-2{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2-2(0) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=10,\\,{y}_{2}=2,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=10+2=12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/94/941d698059cfd145f263cbdde048fc4a32245cdad4bc6bb6d7a504d005b6c231.png)"} :step/id #uuid "b732af76-fd32-53cd-8bfd-76e56549c550" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548183 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be915f26-a2fb-5d29-9b1c-a6e737760c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547721} :answer-field/id #uuid "55faa739-ebaa-5c90-a047-3bc90cd8f091" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f8e2a0-ea50-50ee-b075-78ac59d58fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547821} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c1656d-f914-59ef-862b-d658127e5cce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0dbc8468-e68d-529c-9edc-f8593cda9fbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-250075" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x-1$ and the line $y=-2x-13$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x-1 \\\\ y=-2x-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-1 & =-2x-13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-2{x}_{1}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-4)-13 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,-5).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =-2{x}_{2}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-3)-13 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-3,-7).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/78/782a5e0fbe4c362ca17e86a11f017f6909873cb1f06f0d801d6d958bd1c8ab44.png)"} :step/id #uuid "edd9acce-a217-5a8d-b05c-3997b1f324d3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548149}}] :activity/title "Finding Intersections of Lines and Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547304}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570852} :activity/id #uuid "9cd39b2d-0f29-5922-b59d-7c9d35296ab3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049837 :topic/id #uuid "17cac727-fab1-5837-8069-a3e9364662a5" :topic/math-academy-id 6341} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3399484a-1e9d-5f6e-9670-2f57b635951b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570216} :answer-field/id #uuid "21fc663a-42dd-5170-baa9-33270847b513" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570592} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaa25441-f037-5889-b8ee-98ed9ac7af8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570426} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb279d1-a081-5445-83f9-b74769bc07da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "768c8258-584d-53be-9ed2-7212820baebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-250171" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+3k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+3k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y &=x+3k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =x+3k \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4(1)(-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-16 \\\\ k & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "4096c219-d4f7-5387-84d1-ee267dbd82e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31701e1b-752e-53b5-87f8-c950c55efe89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570209} {:answer/id #uuid "79ab7361-c55a-518b-a40b-db0377da228a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k<-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570340} {:answer/id #uuid "af5a6d7b-5c27-5373-b0c3-f3df6c003fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570441} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8b0a8-1117-56ef-a3e6-79add5f0af91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570459} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3e5360-5da9-5043-989a-9ac296e89f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k<-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570568} :answer-field/id #uuid "7734cd7d-32e7-59ba-99af-993307dacc38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03601169-5838-5406-9812-fc2ccaab4423" :question/math-academy-id "q-155959" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+2k$ intersects the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}-x \\\\ y &=x+2k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-x & =x+2k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=2,\\,b=-2,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4+16k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+16k & <0 \\\\ 16k & <-4 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{4}{16} \\\\ k & <-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\frac{1}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "800ad617-00fa-53b6-9712-0876d2b3638a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570814}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12845067-a8b7-5913-979e-1299091f1d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570159} {:answer/id #uuid "16f6109b-7508-5135-8923-7bab55919240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570170} {:answer/id #uuid "c55f1198-aad6-521b-bae5-b64244ff77e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570468} {:answer/id #uuid "cef3c9ba-eb28-5a61-91c0-f5a03b59dee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570483} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b47cc5-e313-59aa-949c-897284af5182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570558} :answer-field/id #uuid "15617f41-35a9-548a-b217-4bc5608003fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5fa4d8a0-3fad-569b-a952-bc2efe6c446d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152269" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=21-4x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}-{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2} \\\\ y &=21-4x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =21-4x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-21 & =0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-7,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(-7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,49).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{2} \\\\  & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,9).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=49,\\,{y}_{2}=9,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}=49-9=40.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca9ba76d66d80a0a564fbefd02342d3f63d99f6fbd907b736cdc9acee79b174.png)"} :step/id #uuid "b5c66e70-a6e2-575d-9123-3d8008a49c0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40949b7c-a27c-5611-b404-6149a0b8d050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570233} {:answer/id #uuid "4d93872e-5c7a-54f9-bdf4-ce3a1167345a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570262} {:answer/id #uuid "adcb1ac8-48fc-566e-a31f-2be7b6ee6dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570434} {:answer/id #uuid "b4792ac3-9031-5945-8164-12455d78b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570449} {:answer/id #uuid "d37f8a21-89a6-581f-bd81-65529db39d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570496} :answer-field/id #uuid "978d262c-3412-5d34-a3c8-3416c0e3c873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1735602f-bd75-58b9-8473-899a4cca6ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152280" :question/problem "The parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and the line $y=2x+1$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y &=2x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2{x}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =2(0)+1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2{x}_{2}+1 \\\\  & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=3,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1+3=4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7751473a1f9c40086a3a765487cf24f8e5430150d015cd64f1ed0c5bd62fcee4.png)"} :step/id #uuid "e5ad979e-ed88-5992-82ff-1d7fe7e01451" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f0c16c-499b-5a14-9e19-173e4d918a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570175} {:answer/id #uuid "1db6d43d-85f2-5fa4-ae72-336840f36edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570180} {:answer/id #uuid "5594fa57-8458-511c-9aeb-7fe140e03b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570275} {:answer/id #uuid "691119d4-06b9-55bd-8f14-b3a8e2f8e06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570310} {:answer/id #uuid "abc497cf-9291-5541-a458-1cc9c303bee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570310} :answer-field/id #uuid "91cdd488-37fe-52d7-879d-76d1056cf280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "569846cc-609e-5d75-9d24-71aff484c31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-155535" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+3x-1$ and the line $y=-3x-10$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+3x-1 \\\\ y &=-3x-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =-3x-10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-1+3x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-10 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-10 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(-3,-1).$ Therefore, $p=-3,\\,q=-1,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=-3+(-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/91/91bedb41cd1b30cf392b23c0986e7b191ecf170fbdd4a0c34f5e2b8977c747b2.png)"} :step/id #uuid "fdd51c38-56e7-5039-8456-0a3511d2b17e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570838}}] :activity/title "Finding Intersections of Lines and Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570109}]}
